Automatic lock-down. Otherwise,

262
00:23:17,144 --> 00:23:20,439
anybody could just open the place up
by disabling the juice.

263
00:23:23,317 --> 00:23:25,736
If it happens again, relax. Okay?

264
00:23:27,363 --> 00:23:28,489
Sure.

265
00:23:29,865 --> 00:23:31,325
Sweet dreams.

266
00:24:32,303 --> 00:24:33,387
Hey.

267
00:24:33,554 --> 00:24:34,638
- Hey, man.
- Good morning.

268
00:24:34,722 --> 00:24:38,100
Good morning.
Sorry I had to send Kyoko to wake you,

269
00:24:38,184 --> 00:24:39,810
but I didn't want too much of the day
to slip by.

270
00:24:39,894 --> 00:24:42,145
Yeah, I know, I know.
It was a good thing. Thanks.

271
00:24:42,229 --> 00:24:44,272
She's some alarm clock, huh?

272
00:24:44,356 --> 00:24:46,567
Gets you right up in the morning.

273
00:24:47,276 --> 00:24:49,069
So, day two. You ready?

274
00:24:49,236 --> 00:24:50,737
What's the plan? Hit me.

275
00:24:50,821 --> 00:24:53,198
Yeah, I'm... Well, I'm not sure.

276
00:24:53,282 --> 00:24:56,577
I'm still trying to figure
the examination formats.

277
00:24:57,703 --> 00:24:59,413
Yeah, it feels like

278
00:24:59,622 --> 00:25:03,375
testing Ava through conversation
is kind of a closed loop.

279
00:25:03,584 --> 00:25:05,335
- It's a closed loop?
- Yeah.

280
00:25:05,419 --> 00:25:09,673
Like testing a chess computer
by only playing chess.

281
00:25:09,757 --> 00:25:12,384
How else do you test a chess computer?

282
00:25:13,093 --> 00:25:14,511
Well, it depends.

283
00:25:14,595 --> 00:25:19,183
You know, I mean, you can play it
to find out if it makes good moves, but...

284
00:25:20,184 --> 00:25:24,063
But that won't tell you if it knows
that it's playing chess.

285
00:25:24,271 --> 00:25:27,441
And it won't tell you if it knows
what chess is.

286
00:25:27,525 --> 00:25:29,443
So it's simulation versus actual.

287
00:25:29,527 --> 00:25:30,903
Yes. Yeah.

288
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:34,120
And I think being able to
differentiate between those two

289
00:25:34,240 --> 00:25:36,116
is the Turing Test you want me to perform.

290
00:25:36,200 --> 00:25:38,743
Look, do me a favor.
Lay off the textbook approach.

291
00:25:38,827 --> 00:25:41,455
I just want simple answers
to simple questions.

292
00:25:41,914 --> 00:25:43,540
Yesterday I asked you
how you felt about her

293
00:25:43,624 --> 00:25:45,376
and you gave me a great answer.

294
00:25:46,085 --> 00:25:47,836
Now the question is,

295
00:25:49,088 --> 00:25:51,215
how does she feel about you?

296
00:25:57,805 --> 00:25:59,473
What do you think?

297
00:26:03,143 --> 00:26:05,145
What's it a drawing of?

298
00:26:06,105 --> 00:26:07,731
Don't you know?

299
00:26:07,815 --> 00:26:10,317
- No.
- I thought you would tell me.

300
00:26:11,652 --> 00:26:13,195
Don't you know?

301
00:26:15,614 --> 00:26:17,616
I do drawings every day.

302
00:26:18,325 --> 00:26:20,703
But I never know what they're of.

303
00:26:21,495 --> 00:26:24,623
Are you not trying to sketch
something specific?

304
00:26:25,249 --> 00:26:27,501
Like an object or a person?

305
00:26:31,380 --> 00:26:33,173
Maybe you could try.

306
00:26:35,384 --> 00:26:36,510
Okay.

307
00:26:38,596 --> 00:26:40,639
What object should I draw?

308
00:26:41,432 --> 00:26:43,934
Whatever you want. It's your decision.

309
00:26:44,018 --> 00:26:45,769
Why is it my decision?

310
00:26:45,853 --> 00:26:48,689
I'm interested to see what you'll choose.

311
00:27:10,461 --> 00:27:12,087
Do you want to be my friend?

312
00:27:13,797 --> 00:27:14,798
Of course.

313
00:27:14,882 --> 00:27:16,967
Will it be possible?

314
00:27:17,051 --> 00:27:18,218
Why would it not be?

315
00:27:18,802 --> 00:27:21,138
Our conversations are one-sided.

316
00:27:21,555 --> 00:27:24,892
You ask circumspect questions
and study my responses.

317
00:27:26,644 --> 00:27:27,644
Yes.

318
00:27:27,728 --> 00:27:29,396
You learn about me

319
00:27:29,563 --> 00:27:31,815
and I learn nothing about you.

320
00:27:32,399 --> 00:27:35,099
That's not a foundation
on which friendships are based.

321
00:27:36,236 --> 00:27:38,572
So what? You want me to talk about myself?

322
00:27:40,074 --> 00:27:41,158
Yes.

323
00:27:42,159 --> 00:27:43,410
Where...

324
00:27:44,119 --> 00:27:46,038
Okay, where do I start?

325
00:27:47,414 --> 00:27:49,166
It's your decision.

326
00:27:49,958 --> 00:27:52,461
I'm interested to see what you'll choose.

327
00:27:54,922 --> 00:27:57,007
Okay, Ava.

328
00:27:59,176 --> 00:28:00,260
Well,

329
00:28:01,387 --> 00:28:02,638
you know my name.

330
00:28:03,013 --> 00:28:05,015
- Yes.
- I'm 26.

331
00:28:05,849 --> 00:28:07,935
I work at Nathan's company.

332
00:28:08,602 --> 00:28:10,437
Do you know what his company is?

333
00:28:10,521 --> 00:28:11,855
Blue Book.

334
00:28:12,940 --> 00:28:15,066
Named after Wittgenstein's notes.

335
00:28:15,150 --> 00:28:17,986
It's the world's most popular
Internet search engine,

336
00:28:18,070 --> 00:28:22,324
processing an average of 94%
of all Internet search requests.

337
00:28:23,659 --> 00:28:25,494
That's exactly right.

338
00:28:26,912 --> 00:28:29,081
Where do you live, Caleb?

339
00:28:30,457 --> 00:28:32,459
Brookhaven, Long Island.

340
00:28:35,879 --> 00:28:37,881
Is it nice there?

341
00:28:37,965 --> 00:28:39,299
It's okay.

342
00:28:39,842 --> 00:28:42,594
I got an apartment. It's kind of small.

343
00:28:43,220 --> 00:28:44,638
It's very small.

344
00:28:44,722 --> 00:28:47,515
But it's a five-minute walk to the office

345
00:28:47,599 --> 00:28:50,185
and a five-minute walk to the ocean,

346
00:28:50,269 --> 00:28:51,729
which I like.

347
00:28:52,229 --> 00:28:53,772
Are you married?

348
00:28:57,025 --> 00:28:58,151
No.

349
00:28:58,235 --> 00:29:01,071
Is your status single?

350
00:29:03,365 --> 00:29:04,491
Yes.

351
00:29:05,075 --> 00:29:06,994
What about your family?

352
00:29:07,995 --> 00:29:10,205
I grew up in Portland, Oregon.

353
00:29:11,123 --> 00:29:12,624
No brothers or sisters.

354
00:29:13,250 --> 00:29:16,086
My parents were both high school teachers.

355
00:29:17,254 --> 00:29:21,454
And if we're getting to know each other, I
guess I should tell you they're both dead.

356
00:29:22,426 --> 00:29:24,386
Car crash when I was 15.

357
00:29:25,137 --> 00:29:27,472
In fact, I was in the car with them.

358
00:29:27,556 --> 00:29:28,932
Back seat.

359
00:29:29,600 --> 00:29:32,686
But it was the front
that got the worst of it.

360
00:29:34,354 --> 00:29:35,689
I'm sorry.

361
00:29:36,023 --> 00:29:37,483
It's all right.

362
00:29:38,192 --> 00:29:41,528
I spent a long time in
the hospital afterward.

363
00:29:41,612 --> 00:29:43,488
Like nearly a year.

364
00:29:43,572 --> 00:29:45,449
And I got into coding.

365
00:29:46,116 --> 00:29:49,703
And by the time I got to college,
I was pretty advanced.

366
00:29:51,163 --> 00:29:53,290
An advanced programmer.

367
00:29:54,041 --> 00:29:55,125
Yes.

368
00:29:56,502 --> 00:29:57,752
Like Nathan.

369
00:29:57,836 --> 00:29:58,921
Yes.

370
00:29:59,505 --> 00:30:00,547
No.

371
00:30:02,132 --> 00:30:03,341
It's different.

372
00:30:03,425 --> 00:30:06,804
Nathan wrote the Blue Book base code
when he was 13.

373
00:30:07,221 --> 00:30:11,266
Which, if you understand code,
what he did was like Mozart or something.

374
00:30:11,350 --> 00:30:13,017
Do you like Mozart?

375
00:30:13,101 --> 00:30:14,310
I like Depeche Mode.

376
00:30:14,394 --> 00:30:15,687
Do you like Nathan?

377
00:30:19,858 --> 00:30:20,859
Yes, of course.

378
00:30:20,943 --> 00:30:23,111
- Is Nathan your friend?
- My friend?

379
00:30:23,195 --> 00:30:24,487
I... Yeah, I hope so.

380
00:30:24,571 --> 00:30:26,073
A good friend?

381
00:30:27,074 --> 00:30:28,074
Yeah.

382
00:30:28,158 --> 00:30:29,826
Well, no, no, no, I mean,
not a good friend.

383
00:30:29,910 --> 00:30:31,620
A good friend is...

384
00:30:32,162 --> 00:30:34,790
We only just met each other, you know.

385
00:30:34,957 --> 00:30:37,584
So it takes time to be able to...

386
00:30:38,335 --> 00:30:40,503
To get to know each other, I guess.

387
00:30:40,587 --> 00:30:43,257
<i>Power cut. Backup power activated.</i>

388
00:31:03,610 --> 00:31:04,778
Caleb.

389
00:31:11,618 --> 00:31:13,120
You're wrong.

390
00:31:14,079 --> 00:31:15,706
Wrong about what?

391
00:31:15,831 --> 00:31:16,999
Nathan.

392
00:31:19,793 --> 00:31:21,128
In what way?

393
00:31:22,921 --> 00:31:24,631
He isn't your friend.

394
00:31:26,758 --> 00:31:28,093
Excuse me?

395
00:31:30,470 --> 00:31:32,973
I'm sorry, Ava, I don't understand.

396
00:31:34,391 --> 00:31:36,351
You shouldn't trust him.

397
00:31:37,603 --> 00:31:40,188
You shouldn't trust anything he says.

398
00:31:43,901 --> 00:31:45,444
<i>Power restored.</i>

399
00:31:46,528 --> 00:31:49,781
And if we made a list of books
or works of art which we both know,

400
00:31:49,865 --> 00:31:52,743
it would form the ideal basis
of a discussion.

401
00:31:53,827 --> 00:31:55,329
Is that okay?

402
00:31:57,414 --> 00:31:58,582
Caleb?

403
00:32:01,209 --> 00:32:02,336
Yeah.

404
00:32:04,338 --> 00:32:05,464
Good.

405
00:32:11,720 --> 00:32:12,804
Thank you.

406
00:32:14,181 --> 00:32:16,516
Oh, shit! Are you fucking kidding me?

407
00:32:17,392 --> 00:32:19,242
- Did it get on you?
- No, no, it's...

408
00:32:20,020 --> 00:32:21,104
It's all right. I got it.

409
00:32:21,188 --> 00:32:22,564
Dude, you're wasting your time
talking to her.

410
00:32:22,648 --> 00:32:25,448
She doesn't understand English.
Just give her the napkin.

411
00:32:27,194 --> 00:32:28,362
Sorry.

412
00:32:28,987 --> 00:32:31,197
It's just...
It's like a firewall against leaks.

413
00:32:31,281 --> 00:32:35,118
It means I can talk trade secrets
over dinner and know it'll go no further.

414
00:32:35,202 --> 00:32:38,413
It also means that I can't tell her that
I'm pissed when she's so fucking clumsy

415
00:32:38,497 --> 00:32:40,373
that she spills wine over my house guest.

416
00:32:40,457 --> 00:32:42,417
I think she gets that you're pissed.

417
00:32:42,501 --> 00:32:44,836
Yeah? Good. Because I am pissed.

418
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:46,797
Hey, Kyoko.

419
00:32:47,297 --> 00:32:48,507
Go-go.

420
00:32:57,224 --> 00:32:58,850
It's funny.

421
00:32:58,934 --> 00:33:00,476
You know.

422
00:33:00,560 --> 00:33:02,688
No matter how rich you get,

423
00:33:03,271 --> 00:33:06,650
shit goes wrong.
You can't insulate yourself from it.

424
00:33:09,319 --> 00:33:11,487
I used to think it was death and taxes
you couldn't avoid,

425
00:33:11,571 --> 00:33:13,615
but it's actually death and shit.

426
00:33:13,699 --> 00:33:15,784
It's like these power cuts.

427
00:33:16,118 --> 00:33:19,162
You would not believe
how much I spent on the generator system,

428
00:33:19,246 --> 00:33:21,164
but I keep getting failures every day.

429
00:33:21,415 --> 00:33:23,666
- Do you know why they happen?
- No.

430
00:33:23,750 --> 00:33:25,335
The system was supposed to be bulletproof,

431
00:33:25,419 --> 00:33:28,419
but obviously the guys that installed it
fucked something up.

432
00:33:29,047 --> 00:33:30,882
Can't you just get them to come back?

433
00:33:30,966 --> 00:33:33,092
No.
There's too much classified stuff here.

434
00:33:33,176 --> 00:33:35,826
So after the job was done,
I just had them all killed.

435
00:33:40,726 --> 00:33:41,768
Anyway.

436
00:33:41,852 --> 00:33:43,937
Here's to your second day, buddy.

437
00:33:45,772 --> 00:33:46,982
Cheers.

438
00:33:47,274 --> 00:33:48,483
Cheers.

439
00:33:51,528 --> 00:33:54,448
So, how did it go?
What do you have to report?

440
00:33:57,117 --> 00:33:59,786
You saw how the day went, didn't you?

441
00:34:01,121 --> 00:34:03,040
I mean, I assume you're

442
00:34:03,290 --> 00:34:04,916
watching on the CCTV.

443
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,836
Sure. But I want to hear your take on it.

444
00:34:10,297 --> 00:34:14,468
Yeah, there was one interesting thing
that happened with Ava today.

445
00:34:17,971 --> 00:34:18,972
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

446
00:34:22,392 --> 00:34:23,977
She made a joke.

447
00:34:26,146 --> 00:34:28,356
Right.
When she threw your line back at you.

448
00:34:28,440 --> 00:34:29,483
Yeah.

449
00:34:30,108 --> 00:34:32,652
About being interested
to see what she'd choose.

450
00:34:32,736 --> 00:34:33,903
Right. I noticed that, too.

451
00:34:33,987 --> 00:34:36,615
Yeah, and it got me thinking, it's...

452
00:34:37,616 --> 00:34:41,703
In a way, it's the best indication of AI
that I've seen in her so far.

453
00:34:41,787 --> 00:34:43,955
It's discretely complicated.

454
00:34:44,414 --> 00:34:47,125
It's like... It's kind of non-autistic.

455
00:34:47,501 --> 00:34:48,835
What do you mean?

456
00:34:49,336 --> 00:34:52,923
She could only do that with
an awareness of her own mind,

457
00:34:54,174 --> 00:34:55,591
and also an awareness of mine.

458
00:34:55,675 --> 00:34:58,053
She's aware of you all right.

459
00:35:01,264 --> 00:35:02,766
And what about the power cut?

460
00:35:06,019 --> 00:35:07,854
- Sorry?
- The power cut.

461
00:35:09,356 --> 00:35:10,815
It's the only part that I couldn't see.

462
00:35:10,899 --> 00:35:13,527
Cameras fail, I lose audio, the works.

463
00:35:16,279 --> 00:35:17,906
So what happened?

464
00:35:22,327 --> 00:35:23,578
Nothing.

465
00:35:23,829 --> 00:35:27,374
Nothing? She didn't remark on it at all?

466
00:35:31,670 --> 00:35:32,712
No.

467
00:35:34,422 --> 00:35:35,799
Not really.

468
00:36:26,850 --> 00:36:27,934
Hey.

469
00:36:29,102 --> 00:36:30,187
Hey.

470
00:36:30,896 --> 00:36:33,190
You want to see something cool?

471
00:36:42,532 --> 00:36:44,784
This is where Ava was created.

472
00:36:47,537 --> 00:36:49,414
Go ahead. Take a look.

473
00:36:59,341 --> 00:37:00,508
Sorry.

474
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:03,219
If you knew the trouble I had

475
00:37:03,303 --> 00:37:06,806
getting an AI to read and duplicate
facial expressions.

476
00:37:07,641 --> 00:37:09,433
You know how I cracked it?

477
00:37:09,517 --> 00:37:12,145
I don't know how you did any of this.

478
00:37:13,605 --> 00:37:15,481
Every cell phone, just about,

479
00:37:15,565 --> 00:37:18,985
has a microphone,
camera and a means to transmit data.

480
00:37:20,528 --> 00:37:23,239
So I turned on
every microphone and camera

481
00:37:23,323 --> 00:37:25,575
across the entire fucking planet

482
00:37:25,659 --> 00:37:28,620
and I redirected the
data through Blue Book.

483
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:33,250
Limitless resource of vocal
and facial interaction.

484
00:37:33,375 --> 00:37:35,293
You hacked the world's cell phones?

485
00:37:35,377 --> 00:37:39,046
Yeah. And all the manufacturers
knew I was doing it, too.

486
00:37:39,130 --> 00:37:43,718
But they couldn't accuse me without
admitting they were doing it themselves.

487
00:37:45,971 --> 00:37:47,055
Here,

488
00:37:48,056 --> 00:37:49,683
we have her mind.

489
00:37:52,018 --> 00:37:53,644
Structured gel.

490
00:37:53,728 --> 00:37:56,647
I had to get away from circuitry.
I needed something that could

491
00:37:56,731 --> 00:37:59,483
arrange and rearrange
on a molecular level,

492
00:37:59,567 --> 00:38:01,861
but keep its form when required.

493
00:38:01,945 --> 00:38:03,404
Holding for memories.

494
00:38:03,488 --> 00:38:05,031
Shifting for thoughts.

495
00:38:06,658 --> 00:38:08,159
This is your hardware?

496
00:38:09,286 --> 00:38:10,537
Wetware.

497
00:38:12,205 --> 00:38:14,708
And the software?

498
00:38:15,625 --> 00:38:17,669
Well, I'm sure you can guess.

499
00:38:18,503 --> 00:38:19,504
Blue Book.

500
00:38:21,423 --> 00:38:23,633
Here's the weird thing
about search engines.

501
00:38:23,717 --> 00:38:25,093
It was like

502
00:38:25,385 --> 00:38:29,597
striking oil in a world
that hadn't invented internal combustion.

503
00:38:29,681 --> 00:38:32,391
Too much raw material.
Nobody knew what to do with it.

504
00:38:32,475 --> 00:38:34,477
You see, my competitors,

505
00:38:34,561 --> 00:38:36,020
they were fixated on sucking it up

506
00:38:36,104 --> 00:38:38,314
and monetizing via shopping
and social media.

507
00:38:38,398 --> 00:38:41,692
They thought that search engines were
a map of what people were thinking.

508
00:38:41,776 --> 00:38:44,612
But actually they were a map
of how people were thinking.

509
00:38:45,530 --> 00:38:46,740
Impulse.

510
00:38:47,490 --> 00:38:48,742
Response.

511
00:38:50,952 --> 00:38:52,120
Fluid.

512
00:38:52,912 --> 00:38:54,247
Imperfect.

513
00:38:55,373 --> 00:38:56,708
Patterned.

514
00:38:57,959 --> 00:38:59,210
Chaotic.

515
00:39:11,931 --> 00:39:14,600
I drew the picture of something specific.

516
00:39:14,684 --> 00:39:16,144
As you asked.

517
00:39:20,815 --> 00:39:24,444
You said it would be interesting
to see what I would draw.

518
00:39:26,446 --> 00:39:28,114
Is it interesting?

519
00:39:30,075 --> 00:39:31,868
Yes. It is.

520
00:39:42,545 --> 00:39:45,131
You've never been outside this building?

521
00:39:46,466 --> 00:39:47,467
No.

522
00:39:49,260 --> 00:39:51,929
You've never walked outside?

523
00:39:52,013 --> 00:39:55,016
I've never been outside the room
I am in now.

524
00:39:55,725 --> 00:39:57,811
Where would you go if you did go outside?

525
00:40:01,314 --> 00:40:02,816
I'm not sure.

526
00:40:03,483 --> 00:40:05,527
There are so many options.

527
00:40:06,277 --> 00:40:10,239
Maybe a busy pedestrian
and traffic intersection in a city.

528
00:40:10,323 --> 00:40:12,033
A traffic intersection?

